facet.library/EndCommandBuffer                  facet.library/EndCommandBuffer

   NAME
	EndCommandBuffer -- The command buffer's recording ended: it can be submitted.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn EndCommandBuffer(base: *FacetBase, buffer: *facet.CommandBuffer) facet.Result

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -144.

   INPUTS
	- `buffer` - a command buffer recording.

   RESULT
	`success`; `error_out_of_host_memory` when memory ran out for a command
	while it recorded; `error_feature_not_present` when a command asked for
	more than the library draws, as CmdDraw does of more vertices than a draw
	works. Either way the buffer cannot be submitted, and is recorded again
	after a reset; `error_initialization_failed` for a buffer not recording.

   BEHAVIOR
	The buffer is executable: QueueSubmit plays it, as often as it is submitted.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The buffer stays its pool's.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`BeginCommandBuffer`, `QueueSubmit`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (fb.EndCommandBuffer(commands) != .success) return error.NoRecording;
facet.library/EnumeratePhysicalDevices  facet.library/EnumeratePhysicalDevices

   NAME
	EnumeratePhysicalDevices -- The instance's physical devices.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn EnumeratePhysicalDevices(base: *FacetBase, instance: *facet.Instance, count: *u32, devices: ?[*]*facet.PhysicalDevice) facet.Result

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `instance` - the instance.
	- `count` - with `devices`, how many it has room for; written with
	  how many there are, or with how many it was given.
	- `devices` - where they go; null to count them alone.

   RESULT
	`success`, or `incomplete` when `devices` was too short for them all.

   BEHAVIOR
	The machine's cores come first, drawing through the rasterizer. On a
	machine with a GPU facet.library drives - the V3D of a Raspberry Pi 5 or
	a Compute Module 5 - that follows, which draws with fixed forms of
	fragment function alone (`shader.fixedFragment`). A program whose
	fragment functions are all of those takes the last device listed, and
	has the GPU where there is one and the cores where not.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	A physical device is the instance's, and goes with it.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`GetPhysicalDeviceProperties`, `CreateDevice`

   EXAMPLES
	    var count: u32 = 1;
	    var physical: [1]*facet.PhysicalDevice = undefined;
	    _ = fb.EnumeratePhysicalDevices(instance, &count, &physical);
	    if (count == 0) return error.NoDevice;
facet.library/FlushMappedMemoryRanges    facet.library/FlushMappedMemoryRanges

   NAME
	FlushMappedMemoryRanges -- What the host wrote through a mapping, in `ranges`, made the device's to read: nothing to do, every memory type being coherent.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FlushMappedMemoryRanges(base: *FacetBase, device: *facet.Device, range_count: u32, ranges: [*]const facet.MappedMemoryRange) facet.Result

   SINCE
	1.123. LVO -528.

   INPUTS
	- `device` - the device the blocks were allocated from.
	- `range_count`, `ranges` - the ranges, each of a block mapped now:
	  `size` bytes from `offset`, which counts from the block's start, or
	  to the end of the mapping for `WHOLE_SIZE`.

   RESULT
	`success`.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every memory type that can be mapped is `MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_COHERENT`
	on either device, so a write through a mapping is the device's to read
	once the submission that reads it is made, with nothing in between.
	Vulkan has the call all the same, for a type that is not coherent, and
	a program written for one calls it; it is lavapipe's and v3dv's, which
	answer `success` and look at nothing (`lvp_FlushMappedMemoryRanges`,
	lvp_device.c:2390-2396; `v3dv_FlushMappedMemoryRanges`,
	v3dv_device.c:2724-2730). A range outside its block's mapping, or not
	rounded to `PhysicalDeviceLimits.non_coherent_atom_size`, is the
	program's mistake (VUID-VkMappedMemoryRange-size-00685,
	VUID-VkMappedMemoryRange-offset-00687) and is not looked for either.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The ranges stay the caller's, and are not read.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`InvalidateMappedMemoryRanges`, `MapMemory`

   EXAMPLES
	    const range = facet.MappedMemoryRange{ .memory = memory };
	    _ = fb.FlushMappedMemoryRanges(device, 1, @ptrCast(&range));
facet.library/FreeCommandBuffers              facet.library/FreeCommandBuffers

   NAME
	FreeCommandBuffers -- Command buffers given back to the pool they came from.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FreeCommandBuffers(base: *FacetBase, device: *facet.Device, pool: *facet.CommandPool, count: u32, buffers: [*]const ?*facet.CommandBuffer) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -136.

   INPUTS
	- `device` - the device.
	- `pool` - the pool they were allocated from.
	- `count` - how many.
	- `buffers` - the buffers; a null among them is passed over.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	None of them may be pending. What they recorded goes with them.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The buffers are gone.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AllocateCommandBuffers`, `DestroyCommandPool`

   EXAMPLES
	    fb.FreeCommandBuffers(device, pool.?, 1, &commands);
facet.library/FreeDescriptorSets              facet.library/FreeDescriptorSets

   NAME
	FreeDescriptorSets -- `count` descriptor sets given back to the pool they came from.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FreeDescriptorSets(base: *FacetBase, device: *facet.Device, pool: *facet.DescriptorPool, count: u32, sets: [*]const ?*facet.DescriptorSet) facet.Result

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -260.

